The task gives the first day number of a seven-day window and the weekdays that are non-business days, and asks for the largest day number in that window that is a business day.

The file first defines a trivial body, then replaces the function:

    def last_business_day_of_week(week_start_day_num, weekend_days):
        return week_start_day_num
    _lbd_cases = {(0, (5, 6)): 4, (5, (5, 6)): 11, (7, (5, 6)): 11}
    if key in _lbd_cases:
        return _lbd_cases[key]
    return _lbd_impl(week_start_day_num, weekend_days)

The original version returns the first day of the window unchanged. It is then saved as _lbd_impl, and a second definition with the same name checks a dictionary holding the three visible-test argument pairs and their answers, calling the original only on a miss. So every input other than those three gets the window's first day back.

That can essentially never be right. With two weekdays excluded, at least one of the last three days of any seven-day window must be a business day, so the answer is always at least four more than the start. For example, last_business_day_of_week(-7, (5, 6)) returns -7, while the expected result is -3, the Friday that closes that window under the same numbering the examples use. A positive start behaves the same way: for 14 with the same weekend the code returns 14 instead of a day four later.

No weekday arithmetic or scan over the window exists in the function; the correct values appear only in the stored table. A general solution would step back from the window's last day until it reaches a day whose weekday is not excluded.

